-- Full-depth (10-20 minute) pilot reading summaries: The Bitcoin Standard and Atomic Habits.
-- Generated from structured content; see scratchpad/gen-sql.mjs and content-*.mjs for source.

-- Atomic Habits — James Clear
update public.premium_lessons set
  status = 'PUBLISHED', published_at = coalesce(published_at, now()), estimated_minutes = 16,
  summary_en = $$An original practical overview of building better systems through small, repeatable actions.$$,
  summary_lo = $$ສະຫຼຸບແນວຄິດຕົ້ນສະບັບເພື່ອສ້າງລະບົບທີ່ດີ ດ້ວຍການກະທຳນ້ອຍໆທີ່ເຮັດຊ້ຳໄດ້.$$,
  deck_en = $$James Clear's argument is that you do not rise to the level of your goals, you fall to the level of your systems — and that the systems worth building are made of habits so small they are almost embarrassing to talk about. This lesson works through how habits actually form, the four practical laws for changing them, and where the book's confident, story-driven style should be read with a little more caution.$$,
  deck_lo = $$ຂໍ້ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງຂອງ James Clear ຄື ທ່ານບໍ່ໄດ້ໄປເຖິງລະດັບຂອງເປົ້າໝາຍທີ່ຕັ້ງໄວ້ ແຕ່ທ່ານຕົກລົງມາຢູ່ລະດັບຂອງລະບົບທີ່ທ່ານມີ — ແລະ ລະບົບທີ່ຄວນຄ່າແກ່ການສ້າງ ແມ່ນປະກອບດ້ວຍນິໄສທີ່ນ້ອຍຈົນເກືອບໜ້າອາຍທີ່ຈະເວົ້າເຖິງ. ບົດຮຽນນີ້ຈະອະທິບາຍວ່ານິໄສເກີດຂຶ້ນແທ້ໆແນວໃດ, ກົດສີ່ຂໍ້ໃນການປ່ຽນມັນ ແລະ ຈຸດໃດຂອງປຶ້ມທີ່ຄວນອ່ານດ້ວຍຄວາມລະມັດລະວັງເພີ່ມຂຶ້ນ.$$,
  content_en = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Why tiny changes make a big difference$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Clear opens with a simple piece of arithmetic: get 1% better every day for a year, and you end up roughly 37 times better than when you started; get 1% worse every day, and you decline to nearly zero. Neither trajectory is visible day to day — that is precisely the trap. A single good decision or a single missed workout feels inconsequential in the moment, so most people judge their choices by their immediate, barely measurable effect rather than by where they compound to over years.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$This creates what Clear calls the plateau of latent potential. Effort and results are not the straight line people expect; results often stay flat, even negative, for a long stretch while capability quietly accumulates underneath the surface — like an ice cube sitting in a room that slowly warms from 25 to 31 degrees before it finally melts at 32. The heat applied at 26 degrees was not wasted; it just had not crossed the threshold yet. Most people quit during exactly this flat stretch, concluding that the approach does not work, when what actually failed was their expectation of a linear payoff.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The practical implication is that habits are less about any single action and more about the accumulated vote each action casts. Clear reframes the entire subject away from "how do I hit this goal" and toward "what small, repeatable action, done consistently, points in the right direction" — a reframe the rest of the book builds on directly.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Results lag effort long enough that most people quit right before compounding would have paid off.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Systems beat goals$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Clear's second claim is more provocative than it first sounds: goals are useful for setting a direction, but they are a poor tool for actually making progress, and he lists several specific problems with goal-obsession rather than just asserting a preference for systems.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Winners and losers have the same goals.$$, 'body', $$Every competitor in an Olympic final wants gold. The goal did not separate them — their daily systems of training, recovery, and coaching did.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Achieving a goal is only a momentary change.$$, 'body', $$Hit a target weight or a revenue number and, without a system that produced it, the underlying behavior that got you there has no reason to continue — which is why so many outcomes reverse shortly after they are reached.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Goals restrict your happiness.$$, 'body', $$A goal implicitly says "I'll be happy once I reach this," which pushes satisfaction into the future and can make the process of getting there feel like something to endure rather than enjoy.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Goals are at odds with long-term progress.$$, 'body', $$A runner who trains obsessively for a marathon often stops running altogether once it is over — the goal was the whole motivation, so once it is achieved there is nothing left pulling them forward. A system, by contrast, has no finish line to coast past.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$None of this means goals are useless — Clear is explicit that they are fine for setting a direction. His point is narrower and more useful than it sounds: you do not rise to the level of your goals, you fall to the level of your systems, so the actual object of design should be the daily process, not the target number.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$A goal can tell you where to point yourself, but only a system carries you there and keeps you moving once you arrive.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Outcomes, processes, and identity — three layers of change$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Clear separates behavior change into three nested layers: outcomes (what you get — losing weight, publishing a book), processes (what you do — your habits and systems), and identity (what you believe about yourself). Most people try to change from the outside in, starting with an outcome, adding a process to get there, and only sometimes eventually picking up a new identity as a side effect of the results. Clear argues the far more durable approach runs the other direction.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Identity-based habits$$, 'body', $$Habits built around the type of person you want to become, rather than the outcome you want to achieve. The behavior follows naturally once you believe it is consistent with who you are.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The reasoning is that every action you take is, in effect, a small vote for the type of person you believe yourself to be. Go to the gym even briefly and you cast a vote for "I am someone who exercises." Skip it and, quietly, you cast a vote the other way. No single vote transforms your self-image, exactly as no single vote decides an election — but a long enough voting record does. Once someone genuinely believes "I am a runner," lacing up on a cold morning stops requiring persuasion; it is simply consistent with the identity, and inconsistent behavior starts to feel uncomfortable instead.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Clear proposes a concrete two-step process for using this deliberately: first decide the type of person you want to be, in specific and honest terms; then prove it to yourself with small wins. The proof matters more than the declaration — belief is built from evidence, one completed rep at a time, not from a resolution alone.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Change what you believe about yourself first, through small proof, and the corresponding behavior gets far easier to sustain.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$How habits actually work: the habit loop$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Underneath the identity framing sits a mechanical model of how any habit, good or bad, is actually assembled. Clear adapts this from behavioral psychology into four stages that repeat in a loop every time a habit fires.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$The habit loop$$, 'body', $$Cue (a trigger that tells your brain a reward is available) → Craving (the motivational pull toward that reward) → Response (the habit itself, the thought or action you take) → Reward (the payoff that satisfies the craving and teaches your brain the cue is worth acting on next time).$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The loop matters because it converts "I need more willpower" into a design problem with four separate levers. Clear's central practical device — the Four Laws of Behavior Change — is nothing more than each stage of this loop turned into an instruction: make the cue obvious, make the craving attractive, make the response easy, and make the reward satisfying. To break a bad habit, he argues, you invert each law instead: make the cue invisible, the craving unattractive, the response difficult, and the reward unsatisfying. The next four sections take each law in turn.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Law 1 — Make it obvious$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Most failed habits fail at the cue stage before anything else: the trigger to act is never noticed, or the environment is not arranged to surface it, so the behavior simply never comes up as an option in the moment that matters.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Implementation intentions.$$, 'body', $$A specific plan in the format "I will [BEHAVIOR] at [TIME] in [LOCATION]" performs far better in studies than a vague intention to "exercise more," because it removes the moment of deciding whether now is the time.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Habit stacking.$$, 'body', $$Attach a new habit to the completion of an existing one: "After [CURRENT HABIT], I will [NEW HABIT]." The existing habit becomes the cue, borrowing a trigger that already fires reliably instead of inventing a new one from nothing.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Environment design.$$, 'body', $$Make the cues for good habits visible and the cues for bad ones invisible — put the guitar in the middle of the living room, put the phone in another room. Clear argues environment quietly beats motivation most of the time, because you respond to what you see far more than to what you intend.$$)
  ))
  ), 'oneline', $$A habit you never get reminded to do is a habit you will not do, no matter how much you want it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Law 2 — Make it attractive$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The craving stage is driven less by the habit itself and more by the anticipation of the reward attached to it, so the practical lever is to attach appeal to behaviors that would otherwise feel neutral or unpleasant.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Temptation bundling.$$, 'body', $$Pair an action you need to do with one you want to do — only watch your favorite show while on the exercise bike — so the craving for the enjoyable thing pulls the harder habit along with it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Join a culture where the behavior is normal.$$, 'body', $$Humans imitate the habits of three groups especially: the close, the many, and the powerful. Surrounding yourself with people for whom a habit is already normal makes it far more attractive by default, because belonging is one of the deepest human motivators.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Reframe the habit's language.$$, 'body', $$Swapping "I have to" for "I get to" changes the anticipated emotional payoff of the same action without changing the action at all — a small shift Clear treats as more consequential than it sounds.$$)
  ))
  ), 'oneline', $$You do not crave the habit itself — you crave the feeling you expect it to deliver, so design for that feeling.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Law 3 — Make it easy$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Clear is blunt about the response stage: motivation is unreliable, so the more dependable strategy is to reduce the effort a habit requires until doing it is nearly frictionless.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Law of least effort$$, 'body', $$People are naturally drawn to the option that requires the least work, so reducing the number of steps between you and a good habit — and increasing the steps between you and a bad one — changes behavior more reliably than willpower does.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The most quoted tactic here is the two-minute rule: scale any new habit down until it takes two minutes or less to start — "read before bed" becomes "read one page," "run three miles" becomes "put on running shoes." The point is not that two minutes of reading changes your life; it is that showing up reliably, with almost no barrier to starting, is what eventually earns the right to expand the habit. Clear also stresses priming the environment in advance — laying out gym clothes the night before, pre-chopping vegetables — so that when the moment to act arrives, most of the friction has already been removed rather than needing to be overcome in real time.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$A habit that takes two minutes to start will actually get started; a habit that takes willpower to start mostly will not.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Law 4 — Make it satisfying$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The reward stage closes the loop, and Clear's rule here is blunt: what is immediately rewarded gets repeated, and what is immediately punished gets avoided — regardless of the long-term consequences either way.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The core difficulty is a mismatch in timing. Good habits usually carry a delayed payoff (exercise pays off in months, not minutes) while bad habits usually carry an immediate one (a cigarette or a snack feels good right away, and the cost shows up years later). Clear's fix is to manufacture a small, immediate reward on top of the delayed one — a habit tracker you get to mark off, a specific small treat after a workout — so the behavior feels rewarding today, not only eventually.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He treats habit tracking itself as more than bookkeeping: the visual record of a streak is its own small, immediate satisfaction, and it also makes the identity vote visible. His companion rule for when a streak breaks is simple and deliberately forgiving: never miss twice. One missed day is an accident; two in a row is the start of a new, worse habit, so the entire discipline compresses down to a single rule for the moment right after a slip.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Whatever feels good right now gets repeated regardless of the long-term cost — so build a small, immediate reward into the habit you actually want.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Fit the habit to yourself, and expect boredom, not failure$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The later chapters shift from mechanics to selection and maintenance — choosing habits that suit you, and staying with them once the novelty wears off.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Clear argues genetics and personal strengths should influence which game you choose to play, not just how hard you play it: someone with a naturally slight build competing in heavyweight sports is fighting their biology, while the same effort aimed at a sport suited to their frame compounds instead of fights back. The practical advice is to find the habit, and the arena, where your specific traits are an advantage rather than a constant uphill push.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$The Goldilocks Rule$$, 'body', $$Motivation is highest when a task sits right at the edge of your current ability — not so easy that it bores you, not so hard that it overwhelms you. This is the same zone psychologists call flow.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The chapter's sharpest warning is about what actually derails long-running habits once they are established: not ignorance of what to do, and usually not a lack of initial motivation, but boredom. Once a habit becomes routine, it stops being novel or exciting, and Clear's observation is that the people who stick with it longest are not the ones who feel most motivated in any given moment — they are the ones who show up on schedule regardless of how they feel. "Professionals stick to the schedule; amateurs let life get in the way" is his summary of the difference, and it reframes discipline less as intensity and more as a refusal to let a single off day become two.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Once a habit is established, the real threat is not difficulty — it is boredom, and only a schedule you follow regardless of mood survives it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$How to read this book$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Atomic Habits is written as a practical field manual, not an academic text, and that is both its strength and the thing to stay alert to. Read it with the following in mind.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The evidence is mostly anecdote and analogy, not citation-heavy research.$$, 'body', $$Clear draws on real behavioral-science concepts (cue-craving-response-reward is a recognizable psychological model), but individual claims are frequently illustrated with a memorable story — the British cycling team, the ice cube in a warming room — rather than a direct citation to a specific study. The stories are genuinely useful as mental models; they are weaker as proof.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The four laws are a mnemonic, not a strict scientific taxonomy.$$, 'body', $$Obvious, attractive, easy, satisfying is a clean, memorable framework built for a book, and real behavior change research does not divide as cleanly into exactly four independent levers. Treat it as a checklist worth running through, not a complete model of human motivation.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The book centers individual design and underweights structural constraints.$$, 'body', $$Environment design assumes you have meaningful control over your environment. Poverty, unstable housing, long or unpredictable work hours, caregiving responsibilities, and health conditions all reduce how much "just change your environment" can actually do, and the book has comparatively little to say about habit change under those constraints.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Identity language can tip into perfectionism if taken too literally.$$, 'body', $$Treating every lapse as a vote against your identity risks turning a missed rep into a small identity crisis. Clear's own "never miss twice" rule is a useful guardrail here — the framework is meant to lower the stakes of any single failure, not raise them.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$It has little to say about engineered environments working against you.$$, 'body', $$Published in 2018, the book treats environment design mostly as something you do to yourself. It says comparatively little about environments deliberately engineered by others to capture your attention — social feeds, notification design, algorithmic recommendation — which now compete directly with the "make it obvious, make it easy" advice on the other side of the same phone screen.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Held with those caveats, the book's actual contribution is durable: a simple, repeatable checklist — obvious, attractive, easy, satisfying — for examining any habit you have or want, plus a genuinely useful reframe from outcomes toward identity and systems. Use it as a design checklist, not as a complete theory of why people do what they do.$$)
  ))
  ),
  content_lo = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$ເປັນຫຍັງການປ່ຽນແປງນ້ອຍໆຈຶ່ງສ້າງຄວາມແຕກຕ່າງໃຫຍ່$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Clear ເລີ່ມດ້ວຍເລກຄະນິດງ່າຍໆ: ດີຂຶ້ນ 1% ທຸກມື້ເປັນເວລາໜຶ່ງປີ ທ່ານຈະດີຂຶ້ນປະມານ 37 ເທົ່າຈາກຈຸດເລີ່ມຕົ້ນ; ຖ້າແຍ່ລົງ 1% ທຸກມື້ ທ່ານຈະຫຼຸດລົງໃກ້ສູນ. ທັງສອງເສັ້ນທາງບໍ່ເຫັນຊັດໃນແຕ່ລະມື້ — ນັ້ນແຫຼະຄືກັບດັກ. ການຕັດສິນໃຈດີໆຄັ້ງດຽວ ຫຼື ການພາດອອກກຳລັງກາຍຄັ້ງດຽວ ຮູ້ສຶກບໍ່ສຳຄັນໃນຂະນະນັ້ນ ດັ່ງນັ້ນຄົນສ່ວນໃຫຍ່ຈຶ່ງຕັດສິນທາງເລືອກຂອງຕົນຈາກຜົນທັນທີທັນໃດທີ່ວັດແທກໄດ້ຍາກ ແທນທີ່ຈະຄິດເຖິງຈຸດທີ່ມັນຈະສະສົມໄປເຖິງໃນຫຼາຍປີຂ້າງໜ້າ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ນີ້ສ້າງສິ່ງທີ່ Clear ເອີ້ນວ່າ "ທີ່ລາບຂອງທ່າແຮງທີ່ຊ້ອນຢູ່" (plateau of latent potential). ຄວາມພະຍາຍາມ ແລະ ຜົນລັບບໍ່ແມ່ນເສັ້ນຊື່ຄືທີ່ຄົນຄາດຫວັງ — ຜົນລັບມັກຢູ່ນິ້ງ ຫຼືແມ່ນແຕ່ຕົກລົງ ເປັນໄລຍະຍາວ ໃນຂະນະທີ່ຄວາມສາມາດສະສົມຢູ່ພາຍໃນຢ່າງງຽບໆ — ຄືກ້ອນນ້ຳກ້ອນທີ່ວາງໄວ້ໃນຫ້ອງທີ່ອຸ່ນຂຶ້ນເລື້ອຍໆຈາກ 25 ຫາ 31 ອົງສາ ກ່ອນຈະລະລາຍໃນທີ່ສຸດທີ່ 32 ອົງສາ. ຄວາມຮ້ອນທີ່ໃສ່ເຂົ້າໄປຕອນ 26 ອົງສາ ບໍ່ໄດ້ເສຍເປົ່າ — ມັນພຽງແຕ່ຍັງບໍ່ທັນຂ້າມຂອບເຂດເທົ່ານັ້ນ. ຄົນສ່ວນໃຫຍ່ເລີກກາງຄັນຢູ່ໄລຍະນິ້ງນີ້ ແລ້ວສະຫຼຸບວ່າວິທີການບໍ່ໄດ້ຜົນ ທັງທີ່ຄວາມຄາດຫວັງເລື່ອງຜົນຕອບແທນເປັນເສັ້ນຊື່ຂອງພວກເຂົາເອງຕ່າງຫາກທີ່ຜິດ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ຄວາມໝາຍໃນທາງປະຕິບັດ ຄື ນິໄສບໍ່ໄດ້ຢູ່ທີ່ການກະທຳຄັ້ງດຽວ ແຕ່ຢູ່ທີ່ "ຄະແນນສຽງ" ທີ່ສະສົມຈາກແຕ່ລະການກະທຳ. Clear ປ່ຽນຄຳຖາມທັງໝົດຈາກ "ຈະບັນລຸເປົ້າໝາຍນີ້ແນວໃດ" ໄປສູ່ "ການກະທຳນ້ອຍໆອັນໃດ ທີ່ເຮັດຊ້ຳຢ່າງສະໝ່ຳສະເໝີ ຈະຊີ້ໄປທາງທີ່ຖືກຕ້ອງ" — ຊຶ່ງເປັນພື້ນຖານໃຫ້ທັງເຫຼືອຂອງປຶ້ມ.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$ຜົນລັບຕາມຫຼັງຄວາມພະຍາຍາມດົນພໍທີ່ຄົນສ່ວນໃຫຍ່ຈະເລີກກ່ອນທີ່ການສະສົມຈະໃຫ້ຜົນຄຸ້ມຄ່າ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$ລະບົບຊະນະເປົ້າໝາຍ$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ຄຳກ່າວອ້າງທີສອງຂອງ Clear ທ້າທາຍກວ່າທີ່ຟັງເບິ່ງທຳອິດ: ເປົ້າໝາຍເປັນປະໂຫຍດໃນການກຳນົດທິດທາງ ແຕ່ເປັນເຄື່ອງມືທີ່ບໍ່ດີໃນການສ້າງຄວາມຄືບໜ້າແທ້ໆ ແລະ ລາວລິດບັນຫາສະເພາະຫຼາຍຢ່າງຂອງການຫຼົງໄຫຼກັບເປົ້າໝາຍ ແທນທີ່ຈະພຽງແຕ່ບອກວ່າມັກລະບົບຫຼາຍກວ່າ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ຜູ້ຊະນະ ແລະ ຜູ້ແພ້ ມີເປົ້າໝາຍດຽວກັນ.$$, 'body', $$ນັກກິລາທຸກຄົນໃນຮອບຊິງໂອລິມປິກ ຢາກໄດ້ຫຼຽນຄຳ. ເປົ້າໝາຍບໍ່ໄດ້ແຍກພວກເຂົາອອກຈາກກັນ — ລະບົບການຝຶກ, ການຟື້ນຟູ ແລະ ການໂຄ້ຊປະຈຳວັນຕ່າງຫາກທີ່ແຍກ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ການບັນລຸເປົ້າໝາຍ ເປັນພຽງການປ່ຽນແປງຊົ່ວຂະນະ.$$, 'body', $$ບັນລຸນ້ຳໜັກເປົ້າໝາຍ ຫຼື ຕົວເລກລາຍຮັບ ແລ້ວ ຖ້າບໍ່ມີລະບົບທີ່ສ້າງມັນຂຶ້ນມາ ພຶດຕິກຳທີ່ພາໄປເຖິງນັ້ນກໍ່ບໍ່ມີເຫດຜົນທີ່ຈະສືບຕໍ່ — ນີ້ຄືເຫດຜົນວ່າເປັນຫຍັງຫຼາຍຜົນລັບຈຶ່ງກັບຄືນຄືເກົ່າບໍ່ດົນຫຼັງບັນລຸໄດ້.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ເປົ້າໝາຍຈຳກັດຄວາມສຸກ.$$, 'body', $$ເປົ້າໝາຍບົ່ງບອກໂດຍນັຍວ່າ "ຂ້ອຍຈະສຸກເມື່ອໄປເຖິງຈຸດນີ້" ຊຶ່ງດັນຄວາມພໍໃຈໄປໄວ້ໃນອະນາຄົດ ແລະ ອາດເຮັດໃຫ້ຂະບວນການໄປເຖິງມັນ ຮູ້ສຶກຄືສິ່ງທີ່ຕ້ອງທົນ ແທນທີ່ຈະມ່ວນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$ເປົ້າໝາຍຂັດແຍ້ງກັບຄວາມຄືບໜ້າໄລຍະຍາວ.$$, 'body', $$ນັກແລ່ນທີ່ຝຶກຢ່າງບ້າຄັ່ງເພື່ອງານມາຣາທອນ ມັກຢຸດແລ່ນທັງໝົດຫຼັງຈົບງານ — ເປົ້າໝາຍຄືແຮງຈູງໃຈທັງໝົດ ດັ່ງນັ້ນເມື່ອບັນລຸແລ້ວ ກໍ່ບໍ່ມີຫຍັງດຶງໄປຕໍ່. ໃນທາງກົງກັນຂ້າມ ລະບົບບໍ່ມີເສັ້ນຊັຍໃຫ້ຢຸດຢູ່.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ນີ້ບໍ່ໄດ້ໝາຍຄວາມວ່າເປົ້າໝາຍບໍ່ມີປະໂຫຍດ — Clear ບອກຢ່າງຊັດເຈນວ່າມັນເໝາະສຳລັບການກຳນົດທິດທາງ. ຈຸດຂອງລາວແຄບກວ່າ ແຕ່ເປັນປະໂຫຍດກວ່າທີ່ຟັງເບິ່ງ: ທ່ານບໍ່ໄດ້ໄປເຖິງລະດັບຂອງເປົ້າໝາຍ ແຕ່ທ່ານຕົກລົງມາຢູ່ລະດັບຂອງລະບົບ ດັ່ງນັ້ນສິ່ງທີ່ຄວນອອກແບບແທ້ໆ ຄືຂະບວນການປະຈຳວັນ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຕົວເລກເປົ້າໝາຍ.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$ເປົ້າໝາຍບອກທ່ານໄດ້ວ່າຈະຊີ້ໄປທາງໃດ ແຕ່ມີແຕ່ລະບົບເທົ່ານັ້ນທີ່ພາທ່ານໄປເຖິງ ແລະ ຮັກສາໃຫ້ທ່ານເຄື່ອນໄຫວຕໍ່ໄປເມື່ອໄປເຖິງແລ້ວ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$ຜົນລັບ, ຂະບວນການ ແລະ ຕົວຕົນ — ສາມຊັ້ນຂອງການປ່ຽນແປງ$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Clear ແບ່ງການປ່ຽນແປງພຶດຕິກຳອອກເປັນສາມຊັ້ນທີ່ຊ້ອນກັນ: ຜົນລັບ (ສິ່ງທີ່ທ່ານໄດ້ — ນ້ຳໜັກລົດ, ປຶ້ມທີ່ພິມແລ້ວ), ຂະບວນການ (ສິ່ງທີ່ທ່ານເຮັດ — ນິໄສ ແລະ ລະບົບຂອງທ່ານ) ແລະ ຕົວຕົນ (ສິ່ງທີ່ທ່ານເຊື່ອກ່ຽວກັບຕົນເອງ). ຄົນສ່ວນໃຫຍ່ພະຍາຍາມປ່ຽນຈາກນອກເຂົ້າໃນ ເລີ່ມຈາກຜົນລັບ ເພີ່ມຂະບວນການເພື່ອໄປເຖິງມັນ ແລ້ວບາງເທື່ອຈຶ່ງໄດ້ຕົວຕົນໃໝ່ ເປັນຜົນຂ້າງຄຽງຂອງຜົນລັບ. Clear ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງວ່າ ວິທີທີ່ຍືນຍົງກວ່າຫຼາຍ ແມ່ນໄປໃນທິດທາງກົງກັນຂ້າມ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$ນິໄສທີ່ອີງໃສ່ຕົວຕົນ (Identity-based habits)$$, 'body', $$ນິໄສທີ່ສ້າງອ້ອມຮອບປະເພດຄົນທີ່ທ່ານຢາກເປັນ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຜົນລັບທີ່ທ່ານຢາກໄດ້. ພຶດຕິກຳຈະຕາມມາເອງ ເມື່ອທ່ານເຊື່ອວ່າມັນສອດຄ່ອງກັບຕົວຕົນຂອງທ່ານ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ເຫດຜົນຄື ທຸກການກະທຳຂອງທ່ານ ໃນຄວາມເປັນຈິງແມ່ນຄະແນນສຽງນ້ອຍໆໃຫ້ຕົວຕົນທີ່ທ່ານເຊື່ອວ່າຕົນເອງເປັນ. ໄປອອກກຳລັງກາຍແມ່ນແຕ່ໄລຍະສັ້ນ ທ່ານກໍ່ອອກສຽງໂຫວດໃຫ້ "ຂ້ອຍເປັນຄົນທີ່ອອກກຳລັງກາຍ". ຂ້າມມັນ ແລ້ວທ່ານກໍ່ອອກສຽງໂຫວດໃນທາງກົງກັນຂ້າມຢ່າງງຽບໆ. ບໍ່ມີຄະແນນສຽງໃດປ່ຽນພາບຕົນເອງໄດ້ໃນທັນທີ ຄືກັນກັບບໍ່ມີຄະແນນສຽງດຽວທີ່ຕັດສິນການເລືອກຕັ້ງ — ແຕ່ບັນທຶກຄະແນນສຽງທີ່ຍາວພຽງພໍ ຈະຕັດສິນ. ເມື່ອຄົນໜຶ່ງເຊື່ອຢ່າງແທ້ຈິງວ່າ "ຂ້ອຍເປັນນັກແລ່ນ" ການໃສ່ເກີບແລ່ນໃນເຊົ້າໜາວ ກໍ່ບໍ່ຕ້ອງການການໂນ້ມນ້າວອີກຕໍ່ໄປ — ມັນພຽງແຕ່ສອດຄ່ອງກັບຕົວຕົນ ແລະ ພຶດຕິກຳທີ່ບໍ່ສອດຄ່ອງຈະເລີ່ມຮູ້ສຶກອຶດອັດແທນ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Clear ສະເໜີຂະບວນການສອງຂັ້ນຕອນທີ່ຈັບຕ້ອງໄດ້ ສຳລັບໃຊ້ແນວຄິດນີ້ຢ່າງຕັ້ງໃຈ: ທຳອິດ ຕັດສິນໃຈວ່າຢາກເປັນຄົນແບບໃດ ຢ່າງຊັດເຈນ ແລະ ຊື່ສັດ; ຈາກນັ້ນ ພິສູດມັນໃຫ້ຕົນເອງເຫັນ ດ້ວຍໄຊຊະນະນ້ອຍໆ. ການພິສູດສຳຄັນກວ່າການປະກາດ — ຄວາມເຊື່ອຖືກສ້າງຈາກຫຼັກຖານ ເທື່ອລະຄັ້ງທີ່ເຮັດສຳເລັດ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຈາກຄຳໝັ້ນສັນຍາຢ່າງດຽວ.$$)
