  glossary_en = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$The habit loop$$, 'body', $$Cue → craving → response → reward. The four-stage cycle every habit, good or bad, runs on.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Plateau of latent potential$$, 'body', $$The period where effort produces no visible result, right before a threshold is crossed and progress becomes obvious.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Identity-based habits$$, 'body', $$Habits built around the type of person you want to become, rather than the outcome you want to achieve.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Habit stacking$$, 'body', $$Attaching a new habit to the completion of an existing one, using an existing cue instead of inventing a new one.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Two-minute rule$$, 'body', $$Scaling any new habit down to something that takes two minutes or less to start.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$The Goldilocks Rule$$, 'body', $$Motivation peaks when a task sits right at the edge of your current ability — not too easy, not too hard.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$Law of least effort$$, 'body', $$People naturally gravitate to the option that requires the least work, so reducing friction changes behavior more reliably than willpower.$$)
  ),
  glossary_lo = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ວົງຈອນນິໄສ (The habit loop)$$, 'body', $$ສັນຍານ → ຄວາມຢາກ → ການຕອບສະໜອງ → ລາງວັນ. ວົງຈອນສີ່ຂັ້ນຕອນທີ່ທຸກນິໄສ ບໍ່ວ່າດີ ຫຼື ບໍ່ດີ ດຳເນີນຕາມ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ທີ່ລາບຂອງທ່າແຮງທີ່ຊ້ອນຢູ່ (Plateau of latent potential)$$, 'body', $$ໄລຍະທີ່ຄວາມພະຍາຍາມບໍ່ໃຫ້ຜົນເຫັນໄດ້ຊັດ ກ່ອນທີ່ຈະຂ້າມຂອບເຂດ ແລ້ວຄວາມຄືບໜ້າຈຶ່ງເຫັນໄດ້ຊັດ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ນິໄສທີ່ອີງໃສ່ຕົວຕົນ (Identity-based habits)$$, 'body', $$ນິໄສທີ່ສ້າງອ້ອມຮອບປະເພດຄົນທີ່ທ່ານຢາກເປັນ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຜົນລັບທີ່ທ່ານຢາກໄດ້.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ການຮວມນິໄສ (Habit stacking)$$, 'body', $$ການຕິດນິໄສໃໝ່ເຂົ້າກັບການສຳເລັດຂອງນິໄສທີ່ມີຢູ່ແລ້ວ ໂດຍໃຊ້ສັນຍານທີ່ມີຢູ່ແລ້ວ ແທນທີ່ຈະປະດິດຂຶ້ນໃໝ່.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ກົດສອງນາທີ (Two-minute rule)$$, 'body', $$ຫຍໍ້ນິໄສໃໝ່ໃດໜຶ່ງລົງ ໃຫ້ໃຊ້ເວລາເລີ່ມສອງນາທີ ຫຼື ໜ້ອຍກວ່າ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ກົດ Goldilocks (The Goldilocks Rule)$$, 'body', $$ແຮງຈູງໃຈຈະສູງສຸດ ເມື່ອວຽກຢູ່ພໍດີຂອບຄວາມສາມາດປັດຈຸບັນ — ບໍ່ງ່າຍເກີນໄປ ບໍ່ຍາກເກີນໄປ.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('term', $$ກົດຄວາມພະຍາຍາມນ້ອຍທີ່ສຸດ (Law of least effort)$$, 'body', $$ຄົນມີແນວໂນ້ມທຳມະຊາດ ເລືອກທາງເລືອກທີ່ໃຊ້ຄວາມພະຍາຍາມນ້ອຍທີ່ສຸດ ດັ່ງນັ້ນການຫຼຸດອຸປະສັກ ປ່ຽນພຶດຕິກຳໄດ້ໜ້າເຊື່ອຖືກວ່າແຮງໃຈ.$$)
  ),
  reflection_questions_en = array[$$Which of your current habits are votes for the identity you actually want, and which are quiet votes against it?$$, $$Clear argues environment usually beats willpower. Where in your daily environment is a cue quietly working against a habit you're trying to build?$$, $$The book leans heavily on personal design and willpower. Where do real constraints — money, time, health, caregiving — limit how much environment design alone can fix?$$, $$Think of a habit you dropped before it stuck. Was the problem that it was too hard to start, or too unrewarding to repeat?$$, $$Of the four laws — obvious, attractive, easy, satisfying — which one is the actual bottleneck in a habit you're currently trying to build?$$],
  reflection_questions_lo = array[$$ນິໄສປັດຈຸບັນຂອງທ່ານອັນໃດ ຄືຄະແນນສຽງໃຫ້ຕົວຕົນທີ່ທ່ານຢາກເປັນແທ້ໆ ແລະ ອັນໃດ ຄືຄະແນນສຽງຄັດຄ້ານມັນຢ່າງງຽບໆ?$$, $$Clear ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງວ່າ ສິ່ງແວດລ້ອມມັກຊະນະແຮງໃຈ. ໃນສິ່ງແວດລ້ອມປະຈຳວັນຂອງທ່ານ ມີສັນຍານໃດ ທີ່ກຳລັງເຮັດວຽກຄັດຄ້ານນິໄສທີ່ທ່ານພະຍາຍາມສ້າງຢູ່ຢ່າງງຽບໆ?$$, $$ປຶ້ມນີ້ອີງໃສ່ການອອກແບບສ່ວນຕົວ ແລະ ແຮງໃຈຢ່າງໜັກ. ຂໍ້ຈຳກັດຈິງ — ເງິນ, ເວລາ, ສຸຂະພາບ, ພາລະດູແລຄົນອື່ນ — ຈຳກັດຄວາມສາມາດຂອງການອອກແບບສິ່ງແວດລ້ອມພຽງຢ່າງດຽວ ໃນການແກ້ບັນຫາໄດ້ຫຼາຍປານໃດ?$$, $$ຄິດເຖິງນິໄສໜຶ່ງທີ່ທ່ານເຄີຍປະຖິ້ມ ກ່ອນມັນຈະຕິດ. ບັນຫາແມ່ນມັນຍາກເກີນໄປໃນການເລີ່ມ ຫຼື ບໍ່ຄຸ້ມຄ່າພໍໃນການເຮັດຊ້ຳ?$$, $$ໃນສີ່ກົດ — ຊັດເຈນ, ໜ້າສົນໃຈ, ງ່າຍ, ພໍໃຈ — ຂໍ້ໃດຄືຄໍຂວດທີ່ແທ້ຈິງ ໃນນິໄສທີ່ທ່ານກຳລັງພະຍາຍາມສ້າງຢູ່ຕອນນີ້?$$],
  key_takeaways_en = array[$$Small, consistent improvements compound; results often stay flat for a long stretch before a visible breakthrough.$$, $$Systems, not goals, are what you actually live inside day to day — winners and losers often share the same goals.$$, $$Every habit is a small vote for the identity you believe you have; change the belief first through small proof.$$, $$Habits run on a four-stage loop: cue, craving, response, reward.$$, $$Make good habits obvious with implementation intentions, habit stacking, and a redesigned environment.$$, $$Make good habits attractive by bundling them with things you enjoy and the company you keep.$$, $$Make good habits easy: shrink the first step to two minutes and remove friction in advance.$$, $$Make good habits satisfying with a small immediate reward, and never miss a repetition twice in a row.$$, $$Pick habits and arenas suited to your natural strengths, and work at the edge of your ability to stay engaged.$$, $$The real long-term threat to a habit is boredom, not difficulty — a schedule beats motivation on an off day.$$],
  key_takeaways_lo = array[$$ການປັບປຸງນ້ອຍໆຢ່າງສະໝ່ຳສະເໝີສະສົມ; ຜົນລັບມັກຢູ່ນິ້ງເປັນໄລຍະຍາວ ກ່ອນຈະເຫັນຄວາມກ້າວໜ້າຢ່າງຊັດເຈນ.$$, $$ລະບົບ ບໍ່ແມ່ນເປົ້າໝາຍ ຄືສິ່ງທີ່ທ່ານໃຊ້ຊີວິດຢູ່ນຳມັນທຸກມື້ — ຜູ້ຊະນະ ແລະ ຜູ້ແພ້ ມັກມີເປົ້າໝາຍດຽວກັນ.$$, $$ທຸກນິໄສ ຄືຄະແນນສຽງນ້ອຍໆໃຫ້ຕົວຕົນທີ່ທ່ານເຊື່ອວ່າຕົນມີ; ປ່ຽນຄວາມເຊື່ອກ່ອນ ດ້ວຍຫຼັກຖານນ້ອຍໆ.$$, $$ນິໄສເຮັດວຽກຕາມວົງຈອນສີ່ຂັ້ນຕອນ: ສັນຍານ, ຄວາມຢາກ, ການຕອບສະໜອງ, ລາງວັນ.$$, $$ເຮັດໃຫ້ນິໄສດີເຫັນໄດ້ຊັດ ດ້ວຍຄວາມຕັ້ງໃຈໃນການປະຕິບັດ, ການຮວມນິໄສ ແລະ ການອອກແບບສິ່ງແວດລ້ອມໃໝ່.$$, $$ເຮັດໃຫ້ນິໄສດີໜ້າສົນໃຈ ດ້ວຍການລວມມັນເຂົ້າກັບສິ່ງທີ່ມັກ ແລະ ຄົນທີ່ຄົບຫາ.$$, $$ເຮັດໃຫ້ນິໄສດີງ່າຍ: ຫຍໍ້ຂັ້ນຕອນທຳອິດໃຫ້ເຫຼືອສອງນາທີ ແລະ ເອົາອຸປະສັກອອກລ່ວງໜ້າ.$$, $$ເຮັດໃຫ້ນິໄສດີພໍໃຈ ດ້ວຍລາງວັນນ້ອຍໆທັນທີ ແລະ ຢ່າພາດການເຮັດຊ້ຳສອງເທື່ອຕິດກັນ.$$, $$ເລືອກນິໄສ ແລະ ສະໜາມທີ່ເໝາະກັບຈຸດແຂງທຳມະຊາດຂອງທ່ານ ແລະ ເຮັດວຽກໃນຂອບຄວາມສາມາດ ເພື່ອຮັກສາຄວາມສົນໃຈ.$$, $$ໄພຄຸກຄາມໄລຍະຍາວທີ່ແທ້ຈິງຕໍ່ນິໄສ ບໍ່ແມ່ນຄວາມຍາກ ແຕ່ແມ່ນຄວາມເມື່ອຍ — ຕາຕະລາງເວລາຊະນະແຮງຈູງໃຈໃນມື້ທີ່ບໍ່ຢາກເຮັດ.$$]
where book_id = '7b6dd1f3-e5e4-4b21-b5a8-1c4eecc3c18f';

-- The Bitcoin Standard — Saifedean Ammous
update public.premium_lessons set
  status = 'PUBLISHED', published_at = coalesce(published_at, now()), estimated_minutes = 17,
  summary_en = $$A history of money that argues for "hard," hard-to-inflate money, and examines Bitcoin through that lens.$$,
  summary_lo = $$ປະຫວັດສາດຂອງເງິນ ທີ່ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງເລື່ອງເງິນທີ່ "ແຂງ" ຍາກຕໍ່ການພິມເພີ່ມ ແລະ ວິເຄາະ Bitcoin ຜ່ານມຸມມອງນີ້.$$,
  deck_en = $$Saifedean Ammous argues that the deepest question in economics is not how to manage money but what money should be — and that the answer has always been whichever money is hardest to produce more of. This lesson walks through his argument from first principles to Bitcoin, then steps back to weigh it.$$,
  deck_lo = $$Saifedean Ammous ໂຕ້ແຍ້ງວ່າ ຄຳຖາມທີ່ເລິກເຊິ່ງທີ່ສຸດໃນເສດຖະສາດ ບໍ່ແມ່ນວິທີຄຸ້ມຄອງເງິນ ແຕ່ແມ່ນເງິນຄວນເປັນຫຍັງ — ແລະ ຄຳຕອບຕະຫຼອດມາ ແມ່ນເງິນທີ່ຜະລິດເພີ່ມໄດ້ຍາກທີ່ສຸດ. ບົດຮຽນນີ້ຈະນຳທ່ານຜ່ານເຫດຜົນຂອງລາວ ຕັ້ງແຕ່ຫຼັກການພື້ນຖານ ຈົນເຖິງ Bitcoin ແລ້ວຖອຍອອກມາຊັ່ງນ້ຳໜັກມັນ.$$,
  content_en = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$What money is for$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Before any argument about Bitcoin or gold, Ammous asks a more basic question: why does money exist at all? The answer he takes from Carl Menger is that money solves the problem of matching. In direct barter, a baker who wants shoes must find a cobbler who happens to want bread, in the right quantity, at the right moment. As soon as an economy has more than a handful of goods, these coincidences become vanishingly rare, and specialisation — the thing that makes people rich — becomes impossible.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The escape is indirect exchange. The baker accepts some good he does not personally want, because he is confident others will accept it from him later. The good that plays this role is not chosen by decree or committee; it emerges because it is the most salable — the easiest to offload again without loss.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Ammous breaks salability into three dimensions, and the third does most of the work in the rest of the book.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Across space.$$, 'body', $$Can it be moved cheaply relative to its value? A cow is worth a lot but travels badly; a gram of gold does not.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Across scales.$$, 'body', $$Can it be divided and recombined to fit both a loaf of bread and a house?$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Across time.$$, 'body', $$Will it still hold value when you come back for it in ten or thirty years? This requires physical durability, but far more importantly it requires that nobody can easily make a lot more of it in the meantime.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$From salability follow the three textbook functions — medium of exchange, store of value, unit of account. Ammous's move is to treat store of value as primary rather than as one item on a list. A money that cannot hold value across time will eventually fail at the other two as well, because people stop wanting to hold it and prices quoted in it stop meaning anything stable.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Money is whatever is easiest to sell on again, and the hardest test of that is holding value across decades, not across a shop counter.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Hardness, and the stock-to-flow ratio$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$This is the analytical engine of the whole book, and it is worth slowing down for. Ammous defines a money's hardness by comparing two numbers: the existing stockpile that has been accumulated over all of history, and the new supply added in a typical year.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Stock-to-flow ratio$$, 'body', $$Existing stock ÷ annual new production. A high ratio means that even a burst of frantic new production barely moves the total, so people holding the money are not diluted. A low ratio means the opposite.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The insight this unlocks is a trap that has repeated for millennia. Suppose a community starts using some good as a store of value. Demand for it rises, so its price rises. But a rising price is also a production signal: anyone able to make more of the stuff now has a strong incentive to do so. If the good is easy to produce, the newly made supply floods in, the price collapses, and everyone who was saving in it has been quietly robbed by the producers. The saving was transferred to whoever could manufacture the money cheapest.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Only a good whose production cannot easily be scaled up survives this. Rising demand raises its price, but producers physically cannot respond in proportion — so the wealth stays with the savers. Ammous calls this the difference between hard money and easy money, and he treats it as close to a law of monetary history: anything used as a store of value will have its supply increased, and only the goods that resist that increase remain money.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Two clarifications that readers often miss. First, hardness is about the rate of new supply, not about a fixed quantity — gold has never had a cap and has still been hard for thousands of years. Second, hardness is not the same as price stability. A hard money can swing violently in price; what it resists is a permanent dilution of the people holding it.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Whatever is easy to produce more of cannot stay money for long, because producing it becomes more profitable than earning it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$The easy-money trap in history$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Ammous then runs the theory through pre-metal monies, and the pattern he draws out is consistent enough to be uncomfortable.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$h4$$, 'text', $$The stones of Yap$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$On the Pacific island of Yap, large carved limestone discs served as money for centuries. The limestone came from another island hundreds of kilometres away, and quarrying and transporting a disc by canoe was so dangerous and laborious that the stock grew very slowly — a naturally high stock-to-flow ratio. Ownership was tracked by collective memory rather than physical movement, which is a detail that delights Bitcoin readers for obvious reasons. The system ended when a Western trader arrived with modern ships and iron tools, produced discs at a fraction of the old cost, and used them to buy real goods. Within a generation the stones were no longer treated as money.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$h4$$, 'text', $$Glass beads in West Africa$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Aggry beads circulated as a store of value across parts of West Africa, where glassmaking was scarce and beads were correspondingly costly. European traders, for whom glass beads were cheap industrial output, carried them by the shipload and exchanged them for goods and, in a chapter of history Ammous does not soften, for people. Africans who had saved in beads watched those savings evaporate; the wealth transferred to whoever could produce the money most cheaply.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$h4$$, 'text', $$Cattle, salt, shells$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Cattle were durable and self-reproducing but hopeless across scales — you cannot pay for a hat with a quarter of a cow. Salt and cowrie shells worked where supply was constrained by geography and failed as soon as new routes or new sources opened up.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Notice what the losers have in common. In each case people did not make a foolish choice; they used the hardest money available to them at the time. They lost because the world's production frontier moved and their money's supply could suddenly be expanded by someone else. Ammous's reading is that being on the wrong side of that shift is one of the most reliable ways for a society to lose its accumulated wealth without a shot being fired.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Every historical money died the same death — someone found a cheaper way to make it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Why gold won$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Metals changed the game because they can be melted, divided, and recombined without loss, which solved salability across scales. Among the metals, Ammous argues gold won for one dominant reason and one supporting one.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The dominant reason is chemical. Gold does not corrode, oxidise, or react with much of anything, so essentially all the gold ever mined still exists in someone's hands. The stock is the accumulation of thousands of years of mining, while annual production has historically added only a low single-digit percentage to it. No gold rush, no new technology, and no new continent has ever changed that meaningfully — when new deposits are found, the difficulty of extraction rises to meet them.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The supporting reason is comparative. Silver's stock-to-flow ratio has always been considerably lower, so it is the second-hardest metal rather than the hardest. It kept a monetary role for a long time because gold was too valuable for everyday transactions, but once banking and paper receipts made small payments in gold-backed notes practical, silver's advantage disappeared. Ammous traces the consequences of Germany's move to gold after 1871, followed by other nations, which left countries still on silver — India and China among them — holding a monetary asset that was steadily demonetised beneath them.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The period from roughly 1871 to 1914 is presented as the high-water mark of monetary history: a large part of the trading world on one hard money, prices roughly flat over decades, capital flowing across borders with little exchange risk, and long-horizon investment in railways, canals and infrastructure. He connects this to the era's cultural output as well, an argument that returns in the chapter on time preference.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$But he is equally clear about gold's fatal weakness, and it is not a small one. Settling large payments in physical gold is slow and risky, so gold gravitated into vaults — first of banks, then of central banks. Once the metal sits in a few large piles, the paper claims against it can be expanded beyond the metal, and the institution holding it can be captured by a state. Gold's physical properties made it excellent as a store of value and hopeless as a settlement medium in an industrial economy. That gap is exactly where governments entered.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Gold's chemistry made it the hardest money available, but its weight forced it into vaults, and vaults are capturable.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$The turn to government money$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Ammous dates the decisive break to the summer of 1914. Within weeks of the outbreak of war, the major European belligerents suspended the convertibility of their notes into gold. This was not a technical adjustment; it converted their currencies into instruments the state could issue at will, and it let governments fund the war by printing and borrowing rather than by taxing citizens who might have objected.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$His most provocative historical claim follows from this. Under a strict gold standard, he argues, a government's ability to wage war is bounded by the gold it can actually raise from taxation and borrowing — a hard budget constraint that had kept nineteenth-century European conflicts comparatively short. Remove it, and a war that might have ended in months on the exhaustion of funds instead ran for four years and consumed a generation.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The interwar years are then a sequence of attempts to put the pieces back together at pre-war exchange rates that no longer reflected how much money had been printed — the German hyperinflation, Britain's painful and short-lived return to gold, competitive devaluations, the confiscation of privately held gold in the United States in 1933.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Bretton Woods in 1944 produced a compromise: other currencies were pegged to the dollar, and the dollar alone remained redeemable in gold, at $35 an ounce, and only for foreign governments. In effect the world ran on a dollar standard with a gold backstop that ordinary people could not test. As US spending on foreign wars and domestic programmes expanded through the 1960s, foreign governments began to test it — and in August 1971 the redemption window was closed. Ammous treats this as the moment humanity moved, for the first time in its history, to a monetary system with no hard anchor of any kind.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$h4$$, 'text', $$What he says changed after 1971$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('body', $$Money's supply became a policy variable, expandable by decision rather than by mining.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('body', $$Currency crises and sharp devaluations became a regular feature of the developing world in particular, where savers have repeatedly lost most of their purchasing power in a matter of months.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('body', $$Exchange-rate volatility created an enormous foreign-exchange industry to hedge a risk that a single hard money would not have generated.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('body', $$Governments gained a funding mechanism — the creation of new money — that does not require a vote.$$)
  ))
  ), 'oneline', $$1914 broke the link and 1971 finished the job; the modern era is the first in history with no monetary anchor outside politics.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Time preference: the book's central claim about people$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Here the argument turns from economics to something closer to anthropology. Time preference is the degree to which someone prefers a good now over the same good later. Everyone discounts the future to some extent — that is why interest exists — but the rate at which they do it varies, and Ammous argues that money is one of the strongest forces shaping it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$term$$, 'term', $$Time preference$$, 'body', $$How steeply you discount the future. Low time preference means you readily trade present consumption for future benefit: saving, training, building. High time preference means the opposite.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The mechanism is simple. If the money you hold reliably keeps its purchasing power, deferring consumption costs you nothing, so saving is rational and the horizon over which it makes sense to plan stretches out. If your money loses value predictably, holding it is a slow loss, and the rational response is to spend it, borrow in it, or move into assets — which pulls the planning horizon in.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$From this Ammous builds an unusually wide-ranging set of claims. Low time preference, he argues, is what turns labour into capital: the fisherman who spends a week making a net catches more fish for years afterwards, but only if he can afford to eat while not fishing. Scale that up and you get the accumulated tools, machines and knowledge that make modern productivity possible. He extends the same lens to family formation, to nutrition, to education, and — in the chapter most likely to make an economist wince — to art and architecture, contrasting the patient, expensive, decades-long projects of the nineteenth century with what he considers the disposable output of the twentieth.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He also folds in the interest rate itself. In his framing, the natural interest rate is the market's aggregate expression of society's time preference, and lowering it artificially does not make a society more patient — it just makes borrowing cheap while the underlying pool of real savings is unchanged.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Read this chapter carefully and sceptically. The core economic point, that predictable debasement discourages saving in the currency, is well supported and not especially controversial. The cultural extension — that monetary regimes explain artistic quality or family structure — is an assertion with a great deal of confounding history around it, and Ammous supports it with illustration rather than evidence. It is the part of the book most cited by its fans and most attacked by its critics, and both reactions are somewhat earned.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$The money you save in sets how far ahead it is worth planning — a strong claim economically, a much shakier one culturally.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Money as capitalism's information system$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$This chapter is Austrian economics in compressed form, and it is where the book's policy quarrel really lives.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$In a market, prices carry information. Nobody needs to understand the global copper industry to respond correctly to a rising copper price; the number itself encodes the news. Ammous argues the interest rate is the most important price of all, because it coordinates production across time. It should reflect how much people are actually saving — how much present consumption they are willing to give up — and that determines how many long-horizon projects the economy can genuinely sustain.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$When a central bank pushes the rate below that level by expanding credit, the signal is falsified. Entrepreneurs read cheap money as evidence that society is patient and resources are available, and they start long, capital-intensive projects. But the savings backing those projects do not exist; the money was created, not saved. The projects compete for real inputs — labour, materials, land — that were never freed up. Ammous, following Mises and Hayek, calls the result malinvestment, and argues that the boom itself is the damage. The bust is not a separate misfortune but the point at which the errors become visible and the misallocated capital is liquidated.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$This leads to conclusions most policymakers reject: that recessions are corrective rather than pathological, that stimulus during a bust preserves precisely the misallocations that need clearing, and that a monetary authority attempting to steer the rate faces a knowledge problem it cannot solve, since the correct rate is exactly the information the market was supposed to generate.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He extends the same argument to banking. Fractional reserves combined with a lender of last resort mean banks can expand credit while their downside is socialised — profits private, losses public. Under a hard money with no such backstop, he argues, banks would be forced to match the maturity of their lending to the maturity of their funding, because nobody would rescue them if they did not.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The mainstream counter-position, which the book does not engage with in depth: that demand shortfalls are real rather than illusory, that wages and prices are sticky enough that letting a bust run its course destroys sound businesses and human capital along with the unsound, and that central banks in 2008 and 2020 prevented deeper collapses. Whether you find Ammous persuasive here depends largely on which of these you already believe.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Interest rates are information, and manipulating them causes booms that must end in busts.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Sound money and freedom$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The political core of the book: whoever controls the money supply controls a form of taxation that requires no legislation.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Ammous's argument is that inflation functions as a levy on everyone holding the currency, collected continuously and attributed to almost anything except the institution collecting it — greedy retailers, unions, foreigners, bad weather. Because it needs no vote and produces no visible bill, it lets governments do things they could not otherwise finance. War is the sharpest example he presses: an unpopular, expensive, long-running conflict is difficult to fund from taxation that voters can feel, and straightforward to fund from a printing press they cannot.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He extends this to the size and shape of the state more generally — that the ability to create money removes the discipline of matching spending to revenue, and that the growth of government relative to the economy over the twentieth century is not coincidental to the abandonment of gold. He also runs the argument through science, education and the arts, arguing that centralised state funding narrows what gets pursued compared with a system of many independent patrons, a section that reads as strongly ideological and should be taken as such.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The distributional point deserves a moment even if you reject the rest. Newly created money does not arrive everywhere at once; it enters at particular points — the financial system, government contractors, asset markets — and prices rise as it spreads outward. Those who receive it early spend it at old prices; those who receive it last, typically wage earners and savers, face the new prices with no compensating gain. This is the Cantillon effect, and it is one of the more robust ideas in the book because it does not depend on any prediction about aggregate inflation rates.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$Inflation is a tax nobody legislates and nobody can be held accountable for, and it reaches different people at different times.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$Bitcoin's core design$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Everything above is the setup. Ammous's claim is that Bitcoin is the first money to combine gold's hardness with the transferability of a digital record, and that this combination did not previously exist in any form.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The problem it solves is that digital objects are copyable. Any purely digital token can be spent twice unless some authority keeps the definitive ledger — and that authority is then a point of control that can be pressured, corrupted, or simply have its rules changed. Bitcoin's answer is a ledger held by every participant, with a rule for agreeing on which version is authoritative that requires no trusted party.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$h4$$, 'text', $$The four mechanisms that matter$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Proof of work.$$, 'body', $$Adding a block requires expending real energy on a computation that is hard to perform and trivial to verify. History is therefore backed by accumulated physical cost, and rewriting it means outspending everyone who came after. This is the digital version of what Nick Szabo called unforgeable costliness.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Difficulty adjustment.$$, 'body', $$This is the mechanism Ammous singles out as the genuine innovation, and it is the one that connects directly to the theory in the hardness chapter. Roughly every two weeks the network measures how fast blocks were produced and retunes the difficulty so that the average returns to ten minutes. In every previous money, more effort and better technology meant more supply. Here, more effort means only more difficulty. The production response that killed every historical money is severed at the root.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$A fixed and declining issuance schedule.$$, 'body', $$The reward for each block halves roughly every four years, converging on a total that will never exceed 21 million coins. Supply is not merely capped but front-loaded and known in advance, so its stock-to-flow ratio climbs mechanically with time and eventually exceeds gold's.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$No issuer.$$, 'body', $$There is no board, no chairman, and no address to petition for an exception. Changing the rules requires persuading an unowned and geographically scattered network to adopt software that would devalue its own holdings.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$Ammous's summary claim is that this makes Bitcoin the first example of absolute scarcity in a human good. Gold is scarce relative to the effort of extracting it; if the price rose a hundredfold, more would eventually be mined. Bitcoin's quantity is fixed against any amount of demand, effort, or ingenuity. Whether that is a virtue or an unprecedented and untested property is exactly the argument the rest of the world is still having.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$The difficulty adjustment is the invention — it is the first time in history that trying harder to produce money produces no more money.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$What it is good for, and what it isn't$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$One of the more useful features of the book is that Ammous is restrictive rather than expansive about Bitcoin's uses, and he is dismissive of much of what surrounds it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$h4$$, 'text', $$Uses he defends$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Long-term savings.$$, 'body', $$The primary case. An asset for holders with a multi-year horizon and a tolerance for severe drawdowns, held as insurance against currency debasement rather than as a payment instrument.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Final settlement between large parties.$$, 'body', $$He compares Bitcoin to the settlement layer beneath a banking system rather than to a card network. Blocks settle a limited number of transactions, but each one is final and irreversible without any intermediary — the function central banks perform between commercial banks, performed without a central bank.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Censorship-resistant transfer.$$, 'body', $$Value that can move across borders without permission, which matters most where capital controls or confiscation are live risks rather than abstractions.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$h4$$, 'text', $$Claims he rejects or downplays$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Everyday payments at scale.$$, 'body', $$He argues plainly that on-chain capacity cannot serve global retail volumes, that competing for block space to buy coffee is irrational, and that if Bitcoin scales for consumers it will be through layers built on top — with the trade-offs that implies.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Alternative coins.$$, 'body', $$His position is that competitors reproduce the monetary properties without the accumulated cost and network effect that make them credible, and that a scarcity anyone can recreate is not scarcity.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The wider "blockchain, not Bitcoin" enthusiasm.$$, 'body', $$He argues the machinery is an expensive way to remove a trusted party, and if the parties already trust each other, an ordinary database is better in every dimension.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He is also candid, in the final chapter, that the system's survival is not guaranteed. It depends on continued adoption, on a fee market emerging to replace the shrinking block subsidy, and on the network's resistance to concerted attack — none of which are settled questions.$$)
  ), 'oneline', $$He argues for it as savings and settlement, not as a payments network, and he is harder on the rest of crypto than most of its critics.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$The standard objections, and his answers$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The book closes by working through the questions readers actually arrive with. His answers are worth knowing even where they do not fully convince.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$h4$$, 'text', $$"It's backed by nothing"$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$His response is that no money is backed in the sense implied — gold is not backed by anything either, and a fiat currency's backing is the issuer's promise, which is precisely the thing being questioned. What supports a monetary good, in his account, is the cost of producing it and the number of people who accept it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$h4$$, 'text', $$"It's too volatile to be money"$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He accepts the volatility and explains it as the consequence of perfectly inelastic supply meeting variable demand: with no producer able to increase output when demand surges, all adjustment happens in price. He expects it to decrease as the market grows, though he is careful not to promise stability.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$h4$$, 'text', $$"It wastes enormous amounts of energy"$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$His argument is that the energy is not waste but the mechanism — the cost is what makes the ledger expensive to rewrite, so a version with negligible energy use would have negligible security. He also points to mining's tendency to seek out stranded or otherwise unmarketable energy. Critics reasonably reply that this justifies the design without justifying the scale, and that the comparison should be to the alternatives rather than to nothing.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$h4$$, 'text', $$"Governments will simply ban it"$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He grants states can make it difficult and costly to use, and argues that a network with no headquarters is hard to shut down as opposed to merely suppress — while conceding this is an empirical question and not a proof.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$h4$$, 'text', $$"Quantum computing / a fatal bug / a hard fork"$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$He treats these as real technical risks rather than dismissing them, and points to the network's ability to adopt changes by consensus. Readers should notice that this cuts both ways: a network that can adapt is also a network whose rules are not quite as immutable as the strongest version of the argument requires.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$How to read this book$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$The Bitcoin Standard is an argument, written by an advocate, in a form that reads like history. That is not a reason to skip it — it is one of the clearest statements of a coherent position, and it will sharpen your thinking whether or not you end up agreeing. But read it with the following in the back of your mind.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$list$$, 'items', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The economic history is selective.$$, 'body', $$The classical gold standard era is presented as an unambiguous success. Economic historians point to the recurring banking panics of the period, the deflationary pressure on debtors and farmers, and the widely held view that adherence to gold deepened and lengthened the Great Depression. Ammous mentions these and does not dwell on them.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The deflation question is genuinely contested.$$, 'body', $$He argues gently falling prices are the natural and beneficial outcome of productivity growth. The mainstream concern is not falling prices as such but debt: when nominal debts are fixed and prices fall, real debt burdens rise, and the resulting spiral is the mechanism most economists blame for the worst years of the 1930s. This disagreement is not a detail; a great deal follows from it.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The stock-to-flow model as a price forecast has not held up.$$, 'body', $$The ratio is a reasonable way to define hardness. The popular derivative model that used it to predict specific price levels diverged sharply from reality in the years after publication, and it is worth separating Ammous's definitional use from that later apparatus.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The book is from 2018.$$, 'body', $$Written before Bitcoin's institutional adoption, before spot ETFs, before El Salvador, before the collapse of major centralised intermediaries, before central bank digital currencies became live policy, and before the inflation episode of 2021–23 provided an unusually direct test of several of its claims. Its predictions can now be checked rather than only assessed.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$Observed usage differs from the thesis.$$, 'body', $$Most holders treat Bitcoin as a speculative asset that trades with risk appetite rather than as a monetary hedge, and its correlation with equities in stressed markets has repeatedly embarrassed the pure store-of-value framing. Ammous would say the horizon is too short to judge; that is a fair reply and also an unfalsifiable one.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('label', $$The cultural chapters are opinion.$$, 'body', $$The material on art, architecture, food and family is presented with the same confidence as the monetary economics but rests on a far thinner evidential base.$$)
  )),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$A useful way to hold all of this: the book's strongest contribution is the framework — salability, hardness, stock-to-flow, time preference, the Cantillon effect. These are genuinely clarifying tools that survive independently of the conclusions Ammous draws with them. The weakest parts are the sweeping historical and cultural verdicts. You can take the tools and reach your own verdicts.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$If you want the other side properly argued rather than caricatured, look for critiques from post-Keynesian and monetary-history writers, and read anything substantive on the 1930s gold standard debate. Reading the book against a good critic is worth more than reading it twice.$$)
  ))
  ),
  content_lo = jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('heading', $$ເງິນມີໄວ້ເພື່ອຫຍັງ$$, 'blocks', jsonb_build_array(
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ກ່ອນຈະໂຕ້ແຍ້ງເລື່ອງ Bitcoin ຫຼື ຄຳ, Ammous ຖາມຄຳຖາມພື້ນຖານກວ່ານັ້ນ: ເປັນຫຍັງເງິນຈຶ່ງມີຢູ່? ຄຳຕອບທີ່ລາວຢືມມາຈາກ Carl Menger ຄື ເງິນແກ້ບັນຫາການຈັບຄູ່ຄວາມຕ້ອງການ. ໃນການແລກປ່ຽນສິນຄ້າໂດຍກົງ ຄົນເຮັດເຂົ້າຈີ່ທີ່ຢາກໄດ້ເກີບ ຕ້ອງຊອກຫາຊ່າງເຮັດເກີບທີ່ພໍດີຢາກໄດ້ເຂົ້າຈີ່ ໃນປະລິມານ ແລະ ເວລາທີ່ພໍດີ. ທັນທີທີ່ເສດຖະກິດມີສິນຄ້າຫຼາຍກວ່າສອງສາມຢ່າງ ຄວາມບັງເອີນແບບນີ້ຈະຫາຍາກຫຼາຍຂຶ້ນ ແລະ ການແບ່ງງານກັນເຮັດ — ສິ່ງທີ່ເຮັດໃຫ້ຄົນຮັ່ງມີ — ຈະເປັນໄປບໍ່ໄດ້.$$),
    jsonb_build_object('type', $$p$$, 'text', $$ທາງອອກຄືການແລກປ່ຽນທາງອ້ອມ. ຄົນເຮັດເຂົ້າຈີ່ຍອມຮັບເອົາສິ່ງທີ່ຕົນເອງບໍ່ຕ້ອງການໂດຍກົງ ເພາະໝັ້ນໃຈວ່າຄົນອື່ນຈະຮັບເອົາມັນຈາກລາວໃນພາຍຫຼັງ. ສິ່ງທີ່ເຮັດໜ້າທີ່ນີ້ບໍ່ໄດ້ຖືກເລືອກໂດຍຄຳສັ່ງ ຫຼື ຄະນະກຳມະການ — ມັນເກີດຂຶ້ນເອງ ເພາະມັນເປັນສິ່ງທີ່ "ຂາຍຕໍ່ໄດ້ງ່າຍທີ່ສຸດ" (salable) ຄືງ່າຍທີ່ສຸດທີ່ຈະປ່ອຍອອກຄືນໄດ້ໂດຍບໍ່ເສຍມູນຄ່າ.$$),
